\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate how prompt schema size and inter-type confusability affect zero-shot LLM extraction across three information extraction tasks and four datasets: named entity recognition (NER), sentence-level relation extraction (RE), document-level RE, and event detection (ED).

\subsection{Tasks and Datasets}
\label{sec:datasets}

\paragraph{Few-NERD (NER).}
Few-NERD \cite{fewnerd-2021} provides 66 fine-grained entity types organized into 8 coarse categories (person, location, organization, building, art, product, event, other). The dataset spans diverse domains, making it suitable for cross-domain evaluation. We sample 1{,}000 test instances uniformly across test instances; type frequency follows the natural long-tail distribution of Few-NERD. Evaluation uses token-level text matching. Pairwise analysis covers 1{,}953 type pairs with non-zero co-occurrence out of $\binom{66}{2}=2{,}145$ possible pairs.

\paragraph{FewRel (RE).}
FewRel \cite{fewrel-2018} contains 80 relation types sourced from Wikidata. Since the validation split covers only 16 relations, we combine the training and validation splits to obtain the full 80-relation pool required for schema-scale experiments, and sample 1{,}000 balanced instances (12--13 per relation type, stratified to ensure equal representation). Each instance provides a sentence with marked head and tail entities; the task is to classify the relation.

\paragraph{DocRED (Document-level RE).}
DocRED \cite{docred-2019} contains 96 relation types sourced from Wikidata, evaluated at the document level where entity pairs may span multiple sentences. We sample 1{,}000 instances balanced across 96 relation types ($\sim$10 per type) from the DocRED development set and formulate the task identically to FewRel: given a document with marked head and tail entities, classify the relation. DocRED extends the RE evaluation to a larger schema (96 vs.\ 80 types) and document-level context, testing whether confusability effects persist under longer input contexts and cross-sentence reasoning.

\paragraph{MAVEN (ED).}
MAVEN \cite{maven-2020} defines 168 event types derived from the FrameNet ontology, making it a same-domain schema. We use the validation set (8{,}042 sentences containing 18{,}904 event mentions) for evaluation, as test set labels are not publicly available. The task requires both trigger identification and event type classification.

Table~\ref{tab:datasets} summarizes the dataset statistics, including the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) ranges defined in \S\ref{sec:scs}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrlll}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Task} & \textbf{\# Types} & \textbf{\# Eval Units} & \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{BCS Range} & \textbf{Embedding} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & NER & 66 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.77--0.93 & Qwen3-32B \\
FewRel & RE (sent.) & 80 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.42--0.97 & MiniLM \\
DocRED & RE (doc.) & 96 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.53--0.73 & MiniLM \\
MAVEN & ED & 168 & 18{,}904\rlap{$^\dagger$} & Same & 0.87--0.97 & MiniLM \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Dataset statistics. BCS Range indicates the min--max Batch Confusability Scores across count-curve batches (Experiment~1). Embedding column indicates the model used for SCS/BCS computation. $^\dagger$Trigger mentions across 8{,}042 sentences in 710 documents.}
\label{tab:datasets}
\end{table}

\subsection{Schema Confusability Score (SCS)}
\label{sec:scs}

We introduce the Schema Confusability Score to quantify semantic overlap between types in a schema. Given a type $t$ with name string $\text{name}(t)$, an embedding function $\text{embed}(\cdot)$ maps the type name to a vector representation. The \textbf{pairwise SCS} between types $t_i$ and $t_j$ is:
{\small
\[
\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \cos\bigl(\text{embed}(\text{name}(t_i)),\; \text{embed}(\text{name}(t_j))\bigr)
\]
}

For a batch of types $B = \{t_1, \ldots, t_k\}$, the \textbf{Batch Confusability Score (BCS)} aggregates all pairwise similarities:
\[
\text{BCS}(B) = \frac{1}{\binom{k}{2}} \sum_{i < j,\; t_i, t_j \in B} \text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)
\]

We compute SCS with two embedding models: (1)~Qwen3-32B \cite{qwen3-2025} hidden-state representations (5{,}120-d), which capture the extraction model's own type representations, and (2)~all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \cite{minilm-2020,sentence-bert-2019}, a 384-dimensional general-purpose sentence encoder. Few-NERD uses Qwen3 embeddings because its 66 fine-grained types include same-category pairs (e.g., ``person-artist'' vs.\ ``person-actor'') that MiniLM's 384-d space does not separate well; a cross-embedding check confirms directional consistency (MiniLM $r = 0.172$, Qwen3 $r = 0.138$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_bcs_sensitivity}). SCS uses name-only embeddings as a practical simplification. We validate this choice empirically: name+definition SCS yields equivalent predictive accuracy ($\Delta r$: $-0.023$ to $+0.035$; Appendix~\ref{app:scs-ablation}), and for MAVEN/FewRel prompts that use type names without definitions, name-only SCS directly matches prompt content. We use MiniLM as the primary embedding for BCS computation because sentence-transformers are specifically trained for semantic similarity \cite{sentence-bert-2019}; a cross-embedding sensitivity check using Qwen3-32B causal-LM hidden states confirms directional consistency but with compressed BCS range (std${}\,=\,0.034$ vs.\ MiniLM std${}\,=\,0.081$), consistent with known autoregressive anisotropy \cite{ethayarajh-contextual-2019}.

\paragraph{SCS as a lightweight proxy.}
\label{sec:scs-validation}
SCS measures embedding-space similarity rather than task-grounded confusion, making it a pre-hoc proxy computable without running extraction experiments. Empirical validation confirms the proxy is informative: pairwise SCS correlates with observed type confusion on both NER and RE datasets (Few-NERD $r = 0.112$, FewRel $r = 0.187$, both $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}). However, SCS is a partial proxy; context, type frequency, mention ambiguity, and definition quality also contribute to confusion (\S\ref{sec:pairwise}). Task-grounded confusion scores derived from empirical error matrices would capture these factors but require running extraction first, forfeiting the predictive utility that makes SCS useful for schema design.

\subsection{Experimental Design}
\label{sec:exp_design}

We conduct two complementary experimental paradigms.

\paragraph{Experiment 1: Count Curves.}
We vary the number of types presented in the prompt schema to measure performance as a function of schema size. For each count level, we sample 5 random type batches and evaluate the same 1{,}000 test samples across all batches. At count${}=1$, every type is evaluated individually as a solo baseline. The count levels follow natural partition points in each schema's type hierarchy rather than uniform spacing: \{1, 2, 6, 12, 22, 44, 64\} for Few-NERD, \{1, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 80\} for FewRel, \{1, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 96\} for DocRED, and \{1, 6, 12, 22, 44, 64\} for MAVEN.

\paragraph{Experiment 2: BCS-Controlled.}
We fix type count and vary BCS to isolate confusability from schema size. For each count level, we construct one low-BCS batch, one high-BCS batch, and 25 random batches (27 total). We evaluate on Few-NERD (count$\,\in\{6, 12\}$), FewRel (count$\,\in\{8, 16\}$, both 16-relation and 80-relation pools), and DocRED (count$\,\in\{16, 32\}$). Within-pool 16-relation subsets from the 80-relation pool isolate confusability from pool composition. MAVEN results are reported separately due to a centrality confound (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}).

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\textbf{NER}: text-match F1, where a prediction is correct if the exact text string matches after lowercasing and whitespace normalization, \emph{and} the type matches; partial spans count as incorrect. \textbf{RE}: accuracy (exact match of predicted relation label to gold). \textbf{ED}: relaxed F1, where any token overlap between predicted and gold trigger spans, with correct event type.


\subsection{Model and Inference}
\label{sec:model}

We use Qwen3-32B-FP8 \cite{qwen3-2025} served via vLLM v0.20.2 \cite{vllm-2023} on a single 96\,GB GPU with greedy decoding (temperature${}\,=\,0$, seed 42). Two NER/ED prompt versions are evaluated: v1 requests character offsets (evaluated via text matching); v2 adds format constraints, doubling precision with minimal recall loss. RE uses a single prompt version throughout. Both versions produce consistent degradation patterns; we report both where applicable to quantify formatting sensitivity \cite{sclar-sensitivity-2024} (templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}). Cross-model validation uses Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \cite{llama3-2024}. All experiments use single-seed evaluation with greedy decoding, which eliminates model-level randomness; bootstrap CIs capture the remaining sampling variance from batch composition. Pair-level CIs use percentile bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$); batch-level CIs use $B{=}1{,}000$.

\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

For each question, the answer is \textbf{Yes}, \textbf{No}, or \textbf{N/A}. If Yes, the relevant section is indicated. If No, a justification is provided.

\subsection*{A \quad For Every Submission}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{A\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you describe the limitations of your work?}

Yes. The Limitations section (after \S\ref{sec:conclusion}) discusses single-model reliance, modest pairwise effect sizes, cross-pool slope consistency, factorial interaction tests with TOST equivalence, decomposition scope, and metric scope.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?}

N/A. This is a purely analytical study of schema-induced degradation in existing LLM IE systems. It does not introduce new models, datasets, or deployment-facing artifacts. The findings characterize failure modes and are intended to inform safer schema design.

\item \textbf{Do the abstract and introduction summarize the paper's main claims?}

Yes. The abstract and \S\ref{sec:introduction} state the two-dimensional degradation pattern (count and confusability), the cross-model consistency check, and the negative SDGD result.

\item \textbf{Did you use AI assistants (e.g., ChatGPT, Copilot) when working on this paper?}

Yes. We used Claude (Anthropic) for writing assistance and experimental code development. All scientific claims, experimental designs, and analyses were conducted by the authors.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{B \quad Scientific Artifacts}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{B\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you cite the creators of artifacts you used?}

Yes. Few-NERD \cite{fewnerd-2021}, FewRel \cite{fewrel-2018}, DocRED \cite{docred-2019}, MAVEN \cite{maven-2020}, Qwen3-32B \cite{qwen3-2025}, Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \cite{llama3-2024}, vLLM \cite{vllm-2023}, and all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \cite{minilm-2020} are cited in \S\ref{sec:datasets} and \S\ref{sec:model}.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the license or terms for use and/or distribution of any artifacts?}

Yes. All four datasets are publicly available research benchmarks. Few-NERD is released under CC-BY-SA 4.0; FewRel, DocRED, and MAVEN are publicly released for research use. Models are used under their respective open-weight licenses.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss if your use of existing artifacts is consistent with their intended use?}

Yes. All datasets are used for their intended purpose (IE evaluation). No data is used outside the scope specified by the dataset creators.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss whether the data contains personally identifiable information or offensive content?}

N/A. We use established benchmarks that have been vetted by the research community. No new data collection or annotation was performed.

\item \textbf{Did you provide documentation of the artifacts, e.g., coverage of languages, coverage of domains, etc.?}

Yes. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} reports type counts, evaluation unit counts, domain characteristics, and BCS ranges for each dataset. \S\ref{sec:datasets} describes dataset provenance and sampling procedures.

\item \textbf{Did you report relevant statistics for your dataset(s)?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:datasets} and Table~\ref{tab:datasets} report evaluation set sizes, type counts, and sampling methodology. Count levels and batch sizes are specified in \S\ref{sec:exp_design}.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{C \quad Computational Experiments}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{C\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you report the number of parameters in the models used, the total computational budget, and computing infrastructure?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:model}: Qwen3-32B (32B parameters, FP8) and Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (8B parameters). Inference was conducted on a single RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell GPU (96\,GB) served via vLLM. Total compute was approximately 48 GPU-hours of inference (no training).

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the experimental setup, including hyperparameter search and best-found values?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:exp_design} describes both experimental paradigms (count curves and BCS-controlled). \S\ref{sec:model} reports decoding parameters (greedy, temperature${}=0$, seed${}=42$) and prompt versions. No hyperparameter search was conducted as this is a zero-shot evaluation study.

\item \textbf{Did you report descriptive statistics about your results?}

Yes. All key metrics include 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals (10{,}000 resamples, percentile method) as stated in \S\ref{sec:model}. Spearman correlations are reported with $p$-values throughout \S\ref{sec:count_curves}--\ref{sec:centrality_confound}. Permutation tests are used for slope comparisons.

\item \textbf{Did you report implementation details, e.g., versions of packages?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:model} specifies vLLM v0.20.2 for model serving. Embedding models are named with version identifiers (\S\ref{sec:scs}). Code and scripts will be released upon acceptance.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{D \quad Human Annotators or Crowdworkers}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{D\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you report the full text of instructions given to participants?}

N/A. No human annotation or crowdsourcing was conducted.

\item \textbf{Did you report information about how you recruited and paid participants?}

N/A.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss whether and how consent was obtained?}

N/A.

\item \textbf{Did you report whether the data collection was approved by an institutional review board (IRB)?}

N/A.

\item \textbf{Did you report the basic demographic and geographic characteristics of the annotator population?}

N/A.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{E \quad AI-Assisted Research}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{E\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you report the use of AI assistants?}

Yes. See A4 above. AI assistants were used for code development and document formatting. The research design, execution, and interpretation are the authors' own work.

\end{enumerate}

