\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
The decomposition applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas exhibit centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). High-BCS accuracy converges to $\approx$51\% across models, suggesting a difficulty ceiling. RE's separation may partly reflect its closed-set format; the effect generalizes to document-level RE (DocRED slope $-$0.71). Prompt design modulates but does not eliminate the effect (v2 gap: $-$25.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}). Cross-scale evidence (32B vs.\ 8B) shows Dimension~1 replicates fully while Dimension~2 attenuates on smaller models, dropping to near-zero $R^2$ for NER on Llama-3.1-8B (Tables~\ref{tab:cross_model}--\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). Count-associated degradation may partly reflect prompt length (per-type recall 0.586 vs.\ single-pass 0.368, consistent with lost-in-middle effects; \citealt{lost-in-middle-2024}).

\paragraph{Why does embedding similarity predict LLM confusion?}
SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space. The unconditional pair-level effect is modest ($r^2 = 1.3$--$1.7\%$, as ${\sim}89\%$ of pairs never produce mutual confusion); among active-confusion pairs, $R^2 \approx 7\%$ amplifies ${\sim}3\times$ at batch level (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}), yielding $R^2$ of 22--53\%. The NER U-shape (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}) reflects a dual-task burden absent in RE: partial schemas require simultaneous span detection and type-membership filtering; at full-schema, filtering becomes vacuous and recall recovers, confirming task-specific pathways.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Schema-induced degradation in training-free LLM extraction operates along two dimensions (count-associated recall decline, confusability-associated type confusion) with bounded association in RE but coupling in NER. Dimension~1 replicates across scales; Dimension~2 attenuates on smaller models. BCS enables pre-hoc screening.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Training-free LLM IE.}
Training-free LLM IE spans instruction tuning \cite{instructuie-2023}, code-style schema encoding \cite{knowcoder-2024,codeie-2023}, annotation-guided extraction \cite{gollie-2024,adelie-2024}, schema-driven decoding \cite{slot-2025,oneke-2025}, and constrained JSON decoding \cite{json-schema-bench-2025}. For NER, methods include zero-shot decomposition \cite{xie-zeroshot-ner-2023,promptner-2023}, distillation \cite{uniner-2024}, and bidirectional encoders \cite{gliner-2024,gliner2-2025}. For RE, recent work examines zero-shot relation triplet extraction via prompting \cite{relationprompt-2022} and revisits relation extraction in the LLM era \cite{wadhwa-re-llm-2023}. These methods perform well with moderate schemas (4--20 types) but none investigates how performance degrades as schema complexity scales, nor proposes quantitative metrics for inter-type confusability.

\paragraph{Schema complexity in IE.}
The most directly related work is LLM-PEE \cite{llm-pee-2025}, which partitions large event schemas by type embedding similarity. Our work differs in three ways: (1)~we conduct controlled analysis to understand \emph{why} extraction degrades rather than developing an engineering solution; (2)~our BCS-controlled experiments isolate count from confusability, which LLM-PEE conflates; (3)~our SDGD experiments reveal that merge amplification (not partitioning quality) is the bottleneck in decompose-then-merge pipelines. Specifically, LLM-PEE employs union merge across sub-schema extractions, which our \S\ref{sec:sdgd} analysis shows causes precision to collapse from 0.56 to 0.21 in NER as false positives accumulate across passes; RE avoids this because closed-set classification does not require span reconciliation, explaining why LLM-PEE's decomposition succeeds for relation/event tasks but faces unresolved merge challenges on span-level extraction. Other approaches address schema complexity via hard negative mining \cite{iepile-2024}, schema definition optimization \cite{parse-2025}, multi-agent debate \cite{crossagentie-2025,cmas-ner-2025}, schema-aware decomposition for event extraction \cite{dee-2024}, and active prompting \cite{reflect-learn-2026}; all treat it as an engineering problem rather than isolating the underlying degradation factors. Concurrently, \citet{haystack-to-needle-2025} reduces label spaces via iterative LLM filtering, an engineering complement to our analytical approach. Our framework explains \emph{why} such reduction works: it implicitly lowers batch confusability.

\paragraph{Schema complexity in dialogue and IE.} The Schema-Guided Dialogue dataset~\cite{rastogi2020towards} and SGD-X~\cite{lee2022sgd} document performance degradation with schema linguistic variation. \citet{li2023evaluating} observe count-dependent degradation in ChatGPT IE. \citet{volkanovska2024ner} categorize LLM NER errors including type confusion. The relationship between label similarity and classification confusion has precedent in traditional ML: hierarchical classification suffers sibling confusion proportional to inter-class proximity \citep{deng-2010-imagenet}, label confusion matrices improve text classification \citep{guo-2021-label-confusion}, and classification confusion captures semantic similarity \citep{zhou-2025-rethinking}. Hierarchical label sets reduce class confusion in zero-shot image classification \citep{novack-2023-chils}; enriched type descriptions improve zero-shot NER \citep{cocchieri-2025-zeroner}, both orthogonal to our confusability-aware batching.

\paragraph{SCS novelty.} SCS shares its formal definition (pairwise cosine similarity of type name embeddings) with the partitioning criterion used in LLM-PEE~\cite{llm-pee-2025} for schema decomposition. Our contribution is not the metric itself but the controlled experimental framework that isolates confusability's predictive relationship with extraction degradation, distinguishing it from the engineering use in prior work. The modest pairwise effect size ($r^2 = 1.3$--$1.7\%$) is consistent with standards for individual-difference predictors in behavioral research \citep{cohen-1988-power}, where such magnitudes are considered ``small but real'' effects that compound at the aggregate level \citep{lakens-2013-effect-size}.

\paragraph{Embedding anisotropy.}
Our centrality confound (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}) connects to representation degeneration: embeddings collapse into narrow cones \cite{gao-repr-degen-2019,ethayarajh-contextual-2019}, but local clusters can be isotropic \cite{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}, explaining why MAVEN's single-ontology types conflate confusability with centrality.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate how prompt schema size and inter-type confusability affect zero-shot LLM extraction across three information extraction tasks and four datasets: named entity recognition (NER), sentence-level relation extraction (RE), document-level RE, and event detection (ED).

\subsection{Tasks and Datasets}
\label{sec:datasets}

\paragraph{Few-NERD (NER).}
Few-NERD \cite{fewnerd-2021} provides 66 fine-grained entity types organized into 8 coarse categories (person, location, organization, building, art, product, event, other). The dataset spans diverse domains, making it suitable for cross-domain evaluation. We sample 1{,}000 test instances uniformly across test instances; type frequency follows the natural long-tail distribution of Few-NERD. Evaluation uses token-level text matching. Pairwise analysis covers 1{,}953 type pairs with non-zero co-occurrence out of $\binom{66}{2}=2{,}145$ possible pairs.

\paragraph{FewRel (RE).}
FewRel \cite{fewrel-2018} contains 80 relation types sourced from Wikidata. Since the validation split covers only 16 relations, we combine the training and validation splits to obtain the full 80-relation pool required for schema-scale experiments, and sample 1{,}000 balanced instances (12--13 per relation type, stratified to ensure equal representation). Each instance provides a sentence with marked head and tail entities; the task is to classify the relation.

\paragraph{DocRED (Document-level RE).}
DocRED \cite{docred-2019} contains 96 relation types sourced from Wikidata, evaluated at the document level where entity pairs may span multiple sentences. We sample 1{,}000 instances balanced across 96 relation types ($\sim$10 per type) from the DocRED development set and formulate the task identically to FewRel: given a document with marked head and tail entities, classify the relation. DocRED extends the RE evaluation to a larger schema (96 vs.\ 80 types) and document-level context, testing whether confusability effects persist under longer input contexts and cross-sentence reasoning.

\paragraph{MAVEN (ED).}
MAVEN \cite{maven-2020} defines 168 event types derived from the FrameNet ontology, making it a same-domain schema. We use the validation set (8{,}042 sentences containing 18{,}904 event mentions) for evaluation, as test set labels are not publicly available. The task requires both trigger identification and event type classification.

